#37 — the router carries the client's Authorization header to every write shard
2026-08-22 · rig deploy/compose/timelakedb-router-auth.yml (router :6962 → ingester :6963,
TIMELAKE_DATA_AUTH=required, plaintext) · drill cluster-drill/router_auth_drill.sh
branch router-forward-auth on top of bf4767d
================================================================================

THE CLAIM: a write sent THROUGH THE ROUTER with a valid data-plane token is
accepted by an ingester in `required` mode, one without is refused, one with
a wrong token is refused, and the ingester's own authenticated/anonymous
split attributes the router's clients rather than the router. Before this
change the first write was a 401 — the router forwarded each shard with
`db` and `precision` only — and in `optional` mode every router write was
counted anonymous, which is the measurement an operator flips on.

One ingester on purpose: each node loads catalog/config/tokens.json at boot,
so a token issued on one ingester's console is unknown to a peer until the
peer restarts. That is a separate question from this one and a rig with a
pair would have confounded the two.

UNIT: crates/server/tests/router.rs
  a_write_carries_the_clients_authorization_to_every_shard
  — RED with the fix stashed (ing-a must see the client's credential on its
    shard: left None, right Some("Bearer tldb_not-a-real-secret")), GREEN with it.

Two honest notes on the drill itself, both corrected before this transcript:
  * TIMELAKE_ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD provisions the password but the
    principal is still quarantined until rotated once — the first run got
    403 password_change_required from POST /admin/tokens. The drill now
    rotates, then signs in again.
  * The first version sent the same 50 lines twice (Bearer, then Token) and
    expected COUNT=50; it got 100. Correct: LWW dedup is a flush-time
    property and the live buffer shows both copies. Two tables now.

